{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-251480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An apple is dropped from a height and hits the ground in $0.05$ minutes. Find the distance the apple covered during its fall. **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nDuring its fall, the apple covered a distance of\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-251480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the apple is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- We can find the time it takes for the apple to fall in seconds as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =0.05\\,\\text{min} \\\\  & =0.05\\,\\text{min}\\cdot \\frac{60\\,\\text{s}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =0.05\\,\\cancel{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{60\\,\\text{s}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}} \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 9.8\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =0+4.9\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =44.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance covered by the apple is $44.1\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-251480" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b21e1839ef4500cfa7cbd16e31dd556ac59c4e2e02bf0fed3b4289b1dbad09b4"} {"question" "q-251480" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-251480" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "44.1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-10586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A ball is dropped from a height and hits the ground in $3$ seconds. Find the distance the ball covered during its fall. \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-10586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the ball is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The ball falls for $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(0)(3)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(3)^{2} \\\\  & =0+4.9(9) \\\\  & =44.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $44.1\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-10586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b21e1839ef4500cfa7cbd16e31dd556ac59c4e2e02bf0fed3b4289b1dbad09b4"} {"question" "q-10586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-10586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1.08\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "14.7\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "29.4\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "44.1\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "88.2\\,\\text{m}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-10586" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "44.1\\,\\text{m}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-221581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object is projected vertically downward from a building with a velocity of $0.9\\,\\text{km/min}.$ It takes the object $4\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What is the height of the building? **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nThe height of the building is{{field-1}}$\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-221581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the object in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.9\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =15\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The object falls for $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(15)(4)+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (9.8)(4)^{2} \\\\  & =60+(9.8)(8) \\\\  & =138.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the building is $138.4\\,\\text{m}$ high." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-221581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b21e1839ef4500cfa7cbd16e31dd556ac59c4e2e02bf0fed3b4289b1dbad09b4"} {"question" "q-221581" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-221581" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "138.4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A marble is projected vertically downward from a building with a velocity of $0.3\\,\\text{km/min}.$  It takes the marble $5\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What is the height of the building? \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the marble in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.3\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.3\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The marble falls for $t=5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)(5)+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (9.8)(5)^{2} \\\\  & =25+(4.9)(25) \\\\  & =147.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the building is $147.5\\,\\text{m}$ high." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b21e1839ef4500cfa7cbd16e31dd556ac59c4e2e02bf0fed3b4289b1dbad09b4"} {"question" "q-135099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "147.5\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "205.5\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "270.0\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "49.5\\,\\text{m}"] ["math" "74.0\\,\\text{m}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "147.5\\,\\text{m}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-251483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A billiard ball is projected vertically downward with an initial velocity of $0.25\\,\\text{m/s}$ and hits the ground after $0.025$ minutes. Find the distance the billiard ball covered during its fall. **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nDuring its fall, the billiard ball covered a distance of\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-251483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the billiard ball is $u=0.25\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- We can find the time it takes for the billiard ball to fall in seconds as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =0.025\\,\\text{min} \\\\  & =0.025\\,\\text{min}\\cdot \\frac{60\\,\\text{s}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =0.025\\,\\cancel{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{60\\,\\text{s}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}} \\\\  & =1.5\\,\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.25)(1.5)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(1.5)^{2} \\\\  & =0.375+11.025 \\\\  & =11.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $11.4\\,\\text{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-251483" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b21e1839ef4500cfa7cbd16e31dd556ac59c4e2e02bf0fed3b4289b1dbad09b4"} {"question" "q-251483" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-251483" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "11.4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-251707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A ball is projected vertically down from a height of $4.5\\,\\text{km}$ and hits the ground in $30\\,\\text{s}.$ What was the initial velocity of the ball?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nThe initial velocity was\n{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-251707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The ball falls for $t=30\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- We can find the distance the ball falls in meters as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =4.5\\,\\text{km} \\\\  & =4.5\\,\\text{km}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}} \\\\  & =4.5\\,\\cancel{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}} \\\\  & =4.5\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =4\\,500\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (4\\,500) & =u(30)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(30)^{2} \\\\ 4\\,500 & =30u+4\\,410 \\\\ 90 & =30u \\\\ u & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the ball was  $u=3\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-251707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b21e1839ef4500cfa7cbd16e31dd556ac59c4e2e02bf0fed3b4289b1dbad09b4"} {"question" "q-251707" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-251707" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object is projected vertically down from a height of $0.6\\,\\text{km}$ and hits the ground in $6\\,\\text{s}.$ What was the initial velocity of the object?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The object falls for $t=6\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- We can find the distance the object falls in meters as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =0.6\\,\\text{km} \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\text{km}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}} \\\\  & =0.6\\,\\cancel{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}} \\\\  & =0.6\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =600\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (600) & =u(6)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(6)^{2} \\\\ 600 & =6u+176.4 \\\\ 423.6 & =6u \\\\ u & =70.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the object was $u=70.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135105" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b21e1839ef4500cfa7cbd16e31dd556ac59c4e2e02bf0fed3b4289b1dbad09b4"} {"question" "q-135105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "61.2\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "67\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "70.6\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "75.1\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "80.2\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135105" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "70.6\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-251595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A pear is projected vertically down from a height of $1.5\\,\\text{m}.$ It takes $0.5\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the pear? **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nThe initial velocity was{{field-1}}$\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-251595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The pear falls for $t=0.5\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The pear covers the distance of $s=1.5\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (1.5) & =u(0.5)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(0.5)^{2} \\\\ 1.5 & =0.5u+1.225 \\\\ 0.275 & =0.5u \\\\ u & =0.55\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the pear was $0.55\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-251595" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b21e1839ef4500cfa7cbd16e31dd556ac59c4e2e02bf0fed3b4289b1dbad09b4"} {"question" "q-251595" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-251595" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.55"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A coin is projected vertically down from a skyscraper with a height of $720\\,\\text{m}.$ It takes $12\\,\\text{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the coin?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The coin falls for $t=12\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- The coin covers the distance of $s=720\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (720) & =u(12)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(12)^{2} \\\\ 720 & =12u+705.6 \\\\ 14.4 & =12u \\\\ u & =1.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the coin was $1.2\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b21e1839ef4500cfa7cbd16e31dd556ac59c4e2e02bf0fed3b4289b1dbad09b4"} {"question" "q-135223" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135223" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1.2\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "2.4\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "3.2\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "5.1\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "7.6\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135223" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.2\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An apple is projected vertically down from a height of $0.08\\,\\text{km}$ and hits the ground in $4\\,\\text{s}.$ What was the initial velocity of the apple?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The apple falls for $t=4\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n- We can find the distance the apple falls in meters as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =0.08\\,\\text{km} \\\\  & =0.08\\,\\text{km}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}} \\\\  & =0.08\\,\\cancel{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}} \\\\  & =0.08\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\text{m} \\\\  & =80\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (80) & =u(4)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(4)^{2} \\\\ 80 & =4u+78.4 \\\\ 1.6 & =4u \\\\ u & =0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the apple was $u=0.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135104" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b21e1839ef4500cfa7cbd16e31dd556ac59c4e2e02bf0fed3b4289b1dbad09b4"} {"question" "q-135104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0.1\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "0.4\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "0.8\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "1.2\\,\\text{m/s}"] ["math" "1.6\\,\\text{m/s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135104" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.4\\,\\text{m/s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-31409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A brick is dropped vertically downward from a height of $176.4\\,\\text{m}.$ How long does it take for the brick to hit the ground?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-31409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the brick is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The brick covers the distance of $s=176.4\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ 176.4 & =(0)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 176.4 & =4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{176.4}{4.9} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =36 \\\\ t & =\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the brick hits the ground after $t=6$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-31409" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b21e1839ef4500cfa7cbd16e31dd556ac59c4e2e02bf0fed3b4289b1dbad09b4"} {"question" "q-31409" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e