igned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ 44.1 & =(0)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 44.1 & =4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =\\frac{44.1}{4.9} \\\\ {t}^{2} & =9 \\\\ t & =\\pm 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the ball hits the ground after $t=3$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-10510" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b21e1839ef4500cfa7cbd16e31dd556ac59c4e2e02bf0fed3b4289b1dbad09b4"} {"question" "q-10510" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-10510" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "2.5\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "2\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "3\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "4.2\\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-10510" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\,\\text{s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-221596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "An object is dropped vertically downward from a height of $313.6\\,\\text{m}.$ How long does it take for the object to hit the ground?\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*\n\nThe object hits the ground in{{field-1}}seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-221596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the object is $u=0\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The object covers the distance of $s=313.6\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ 313.6 & =(0)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 313.6 & =4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{313.6}{4.9} & ={t}^{2} \\\\ 64 & ={t}^{2} \\\\ t & =\\pm 8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the object hits the ground after $t=8$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-221596" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b21e1839ef4500cfa7cbd16e31dd556ac59c4e2e02bf0fed3b4289b1dbad09b4"} {"question" "q-221596" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-221596" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A marble is projected vertically downward from a height of $245\\,\\text{m}$ with an initial velocity of $24.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$ How long will it take for the marble to hit the ground? \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the marble is $u=24.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$\n\n- The marble covers the distance of $s=245\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ 245 & =(24.5)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 245 & =24.5t+4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ 50(4.9) & =5(4.9)t+4.9{t}^{2} \\\\ 50\\cancel{(4.9)} & =5\\cancel{(4.9)}t+\\cancel{(4.9)}{t}^{2} \\\\ 50 & =5t+{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+5t-50 & =0 \\\\ (t+10)(t-5) & =0 \\\\ t & =-10,\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the marble hits the ground after $t=5$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135228" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b21e1839ef4500cfa7cbd16e31dd556ac59c4e2e02bf0fed3b4289b1dbad09b4"} {"question" "q-135228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "12\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "3\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "5\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "7\\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-135228" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "5\\,\\text{s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-31411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A marble is projected vertically downward from a height of $88.2\\,\\text{m}$ with a velocity of  $141.12\\,\\text{km/h}.$ How long will it take for the marble to hit the ground? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places. \n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$*" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-31411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the marble in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =141.12\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}} \\\\  & =141.12\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{h}}{60\\,\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =141.12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}\\cdot \\frac{1000\\,\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{60\\,\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =141.12\\cdot \\frac{1\\,000\\,\\text{m}}{{60}^{2}\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =39.2\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The marble covers the distance of $s=88.2\\,\\text{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}88.2 & =(39.2)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 18(4.9) & =8(4.9)t+(4.9){t}^{2} \\\\ 18\\cancel{(4.9)} & =8\\cancel{(4.9)}t+\\cancel{(4.9)}{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+8t-18 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{{8}^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot (-18)}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8\\pm \\sqrt{64+72}}{2} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{-8\\pm 11.6619}{2} \\\\  & ≈-9.83,\\,1.83.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the marble hits the ground after $1.83\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-31411" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b21e1839ef4500cfa7cbd16e31dd556ac59c4e2e02bf0fed3b4289b1dbad09b4"} {"question" "q-31411" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-31411" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1.83\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "2.53\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "3.26\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "4.32\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "5.56\\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "q-31411" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1.83\\,\\text{s}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/state.json" "file_sha256" "d908d34ec9de3f29aba797fe60e99f14165d11e2a58d2a6dfa80d475e6c37e2e"} {"question" "e-1488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A penny is projected vertically downward with an initial velocity of $300 \\, \\textrm{m/min}$ and hits the ground after $2$ seconds. Find the distance the penny covered during its fall." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/example-1488.json" "file_sha256" "a1dcb3f88e8e12609fb89f071ec0b2fc9dbe2fdc8b8397e89c616c537fe961a8"} {"question" "e-1488" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the object in meters per second as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =300\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =300\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =300\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{60\\,\\text{s}} \\\\  & =\\frac{300}{60}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =5\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The penny falls for $t=2\\,\\textrm{s}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\  & =(5)(2)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(2)^{2} \\\\  & =10+19.6 \\\\  & =29.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $29.6\\,\\textrm{m}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/example-1488.json" "file_sha256" "a1dcb3f88e8e12609fb89f071ec0b2fc9dbe2fdc8b8397e89c616c537fe961a8"} {"question" "e-9738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A ball is projected vertically down from a building with a height of $110.4 \\, \\textrm{m}.$ It takes $4\\,\\textrm{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the ball?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/example-9738.json" "file_sha256" "10a16de48a948f1ef612976bc3a304ddc06af9f47454e4b68279f6825f848581"} {"question" "e-9738" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The ball falls for $t=4\\,\\textrm{s}.$\n\n- The ball covers the distance of $s=110.4\\, \\textrm{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s & =ut+\\frac{1}{2}g{t}^{2} \\\\ (110.4) & =u(4)+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(4)^{2} \\\\ 110.4 & =4u+78.4 \\\\ 32 & =4u \\\\ u & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the ball was $u= 8 \\, \\textrm{m/s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/example-9738.json" "file_sha256" "10a16de48a948f1ef612976bc3a304ddc06af9f47454e4b68279f6825f848581"} {"question" "e-1490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A stone is projected vertically downward from a height of $19.6\\,\\textrm{m}$ with a velocity of  $14.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$ How long will it take for the stone to hit the ground?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/example-1490.json" "file_sha256" "b94d92f3f1c97c898c844675c325596e58db871b013b53f04f3f302de40ed47a"} {"question" "e-1490" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the stone is $u=14.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$\n\n- The stone covers the distance of $s=19.6\\,\\textrm{m}.$\n\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2.$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}19.6 & =(14.7)t+\\frac{1}{2}(9.8){t}^{2} \\\\ 4(4.9) & =3(4.9)t+(4.9){t}^{2} \\\\ 4\\cancel{(4.9)} & =3\\cancel{(4.9)}t+\\cancel{(4.9)}{t}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =3t+{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{2}+3t-4 & =0 \\\\ (t+4)(t-1) & =0 \\\\ t & =-4,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the stone hits the ground after $t=1$ second." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039469/example-1490.json" "file_sha256" "b94d92f3f1c97c898c844675c325596e58db871b013b53f04f3f302de40ed47a"}] "usage" {"e-1490" [] "q-221581" [] "e-9738" [] "e-1488" [] "q-10586" []} "basis" 1365 "retractions" [[17592186083393 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA ball is dropped from a height and hits the ground in $3$ seconds. Find the distance the ball covered during its fall.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8m/s^{2}$.*"] [17592186083533 :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nAn object is projected vertically downward from a building with a velocity of $0.9km/min$. It takes the object $4s$ to hit the ground. What is the height of the building? **Do not round your answer**.\n\n*Hint: The acceleration due to gravity is $9.8m/s^{2}$.*\n\nThe height of the building is {{field-1}} $m$.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"solving-for-initial-velocity\"></a>"] [17592186082858 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- We can find the initial velocity of the object in meters per second as follows:\n$u|= 300\\frac{m}{min}; = 300\\frac{m}{min} \\cdot \\frac{min}{60s}; = 300\\frac{m}{min} \\cdot \\frac{min}{60s}; = \\frac{300}{60} \\cdot \\frac{m}{s}; = 5m/s$\n- The penny falls for $t=2\\,\\textrm{s}$.\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= ut + \\frac{1}{2}gt^{2} \\\\\n&= (5)(2) + \\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(2)^{2} \\\\\n&= 10 + 19.6 \\\\\n&= 29.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance is $29.6\\,\\textrm{m}$."] [17592186083055 :question/problem "A ball is projected vertically down from a building with a height of $110.4 \\, \\textrm{m}$. It takes $4\\,\\textrm{s}$ to hit the ground. What was the initial velocity of the ball?"] [17592186083055 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The ball falls for $t=4\\,\\textrm{s}$.\n- The ball covers the distance of $s=110.4\\, \\textrm{m}$.\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $u$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ns &= ut + \\frac{1}{2}gt^{2} \\\\\n(110.4) &= u(4) + \\frac{1}{2}(9.8)(4)^{2} \\\\\n110.4 &= 4u + 78.4 \\\\\n32 &= 4u \\\\\nu &= 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the initial velocity of the ball was\n\n$$\nu= 8 \\, \\textrm{m/s}\n$$"] [17592186082859 :question/problem "A stone is projected vertically downward from a height of $19.6\\,\\textrm{m}$ with a velocity of $14.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}$. How long will it take for the stone to hit the ground?"] [17592186082859 :question/worked-solution "To solve this problem, we use the formula\n\n$$\ns=ut+\\dfrac{1}{2}gt^2\n$$\n\nwhere $s$ is the displacement, measured from the starting position in the direction of the initial motion, $u$ is the initial velocity, $t$ is the time since the beginning of the motion, and $g$ is the acceleration due to gravity.\n\nLet's summarize the information we have:\n\n- The initial velocity of the stone is $u=14.7\\,\\textrm{m/s}$.\n- The stone covers the distance of $s=19.6\\,\\textrm{m}$.\n- The acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}^2$.\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula and solving for $t$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n19.6 &= (14.7)t + \\frac{1}{2}(9.8)t^{2} \\\\\n4(4.9) &= 3(4.9)t + (4.9)t^{2} \\\\\n4(4.9) &= 3(4.9)t + (4.9)t^{2} \\\\\n4 &= 3t + t^{2} \\\\\nt^{2} + 3t - 4 &= 0 \\\\\n(t + 4)(t - 1) &= 0 \\\\\nt &= -4, 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe disregard the negative solution. Therefore, the stone hits the ground after $t=1$ second."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186079689 17592186079690 17592186079691 17592186079692 17592186079693 17592186232632] "immutable_fields" [[17592186079688 :answer-field/correct] [17592186079688 :answer-field/id] [17592186079688 :answer-field/key] [1759218607